GreyKnight the Wizard was roaming the dungeons, as was his wont. It had
been a productive expedition, his loyal kitten Master Sprinkles having
aided him in fleecing several unscrupulous shopkeepers of their
ill-gotten wares, before succumbing to one of the thrice-accursed
kobolds in the Mines of the Gnomes. This had particularly irritated
GreyKnight since, even though he had thought to pack a special wand
that raises the dead to life, the foul beast had left nothing of the
young feline to be resurrected.

Still, there was that sink on the upper levels, and the apprentice
sorceror was eager to try his hand at mastering the oozing creatures
that dwelt within. He had at his disposal a small notebook filled with
scribblings on the care and management of the strange beasts; hints
copied laboriously by candlelight from aged tomes written by those who
had performed such feats before. One of GreyKnight's previous
incarnations had learned the hard way that the puddings would be
hesitant to respect a mere novice, and would gladly chew upon his
bones.

So it was that the young wizard found himself descending past the
enchanted sink, the Mines, delving to and beyond the mysterious domain
of the Oracle. He was waylaid by foul monsters which quickly fell
before the combined onslaught of magical blasts and a hail of tiny
darts liberated from some of the many traps that wait for the unwary
adventurer. After a nasty incident involving two bear traps placed
side-by-side (in which our hapless hero had successfully freed himself
from one, only to blunder straight into the other), he had successfully
disarmed the metal contraptions for his own use. These, plus a plethora
of magical tools found in the corridors and 'borrowed' from shops,
outfitted a wizard more powerful, more formidable than any who had
borne that name before. The crowning acheivement was a pair of
enchanted gauntlets that increased the strength of those that wore
them; they seemed to hinder his spellcasting arm-movements slightly,
but it was of no great matter.

Thus it is that we see him carefully exploring a new floor of the
dungeons. Having been set upon by a swarm of killer bees shortly after
his arrival, GreyKnight inscribes the name of the famed Valie upon the
flagstones to create an impediment to his foes' onslaught, that he
might recover his senses once more. Fully refreshed, and having fed
upon a pie baked with the eye of a lich, he sallied forth once again to
discover the lay of the land. All enemies crumbled before his powerful
assaults with quarterstaff, enchanted gloves, lethal darts, and magical
bolts of force.

Suddenly, he spots a small red creature making its way down the
corridor, intent on murder. A dreaded fire ant! Bane of wizards
everywhere, these beings could easily scorch and burn the precious
tools of his trade! Quick as a flash, GreyKnight unleashed a barrage of
magical fury upon the beast, slaying it not a moment too soon. Panting
from his exertion, he nonetheless realised his increased prowess with
spells of offence. He felt like he could be more dangerous! A slight
pause to refresh his powers a little, and off he set back to the
previous room, to fetch another tasty pie; for this wizard had taken
with him a pair of magical rings that between them protected him and
made him more accurate in his battles, and the drain on his body was
such that he must needs eat frequently.

Disaster struck! During his exploration of the corridor, and the
near-miss with the ant of fire, a kobold - one of that evil, vicious,
unnatural breed - had proceeded via a roundabout route to try and
ambush our hero from behind. Normally this would be of no concern, but
on the vile beast's travels it had acquired an accompanying horde; a
horde of that dreaded foe which no man wishes to see - a horde of
SOLDIER ANTS! As the blue insects swarmed from the opposing archway,
the apprentice pelts them with magical blasts, inbetween retreating to
the safety of the corridor. They are too fast for him though, and
quickly close to biting range! A few whacks from his trusty
quarterstaff mercifully dispatches the leader, and the swarm is
disoriented long enough for him to close the oaken portal and seal it
with a wand of magical locking.

As he sits down and leans against the door, a pair of fire ants
suddenly loom out of the darkness close at hand. In his extremely
weakened state, our erstwhile hero has no choice but to go noisily to
the grave. His dying thoughts are of the great Elbereth, and he wishes
he had thought of her powerful name before it was too late...


